WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Xi Jinping spent the past few years
reining in China's tech giants.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.020 position:59%
Now, as he faces a backlash over his
handling of the Covid-19 pandemic, the

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:17.550 position:61%
Chinese premier is discovering he might
need them. In mid 2020.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.810 position:59%
Alibaba and Tencent seemed on track to
become trillion dollar companies.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.860 position:61%
Then everything changed.
Concerned about their rising influence,

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.510 position:63%
Xi targeted the e-commerce giant Alibaba
for perceived monopolistic conduct

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:35.130 position:69%
and Tencent, which owns the super-app WeChat
for its tight grip on music streaming

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.710 position:58%
rights.
Other tech firms came into the firing

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.570
line, too.
That appears to be changing.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.770 position:61%
Judging by an economic planning meeting
in December, during that meeting, he

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.880 position:56%
signaled strong support for the tech
sector.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:51.420 position:61%
That's because after Covid zero
policies, the Chinese economy is really

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.110 position:63%
tanking.
So right now, he needs a new way to fire

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.750
up the economy again.
That's why he's looking at tech.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.380
Tech has been the Chinese growth
engine for the past decade.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.379
That doesn't mean it's a return to
business as usual for China's tech

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.990
giants.
But life might start to get just a

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:07.410 line:92%
little bit easier.

